    excerpt: {
      workId: "aquinas-summa",
      author: "Thomas Aquinas",
      citation: "De Regimine Principum (De Regno) I.6 (trans. G. B. Phelan, 1938; in public domain editions reprinted from earlier translations)",
      text: "A tyrannical government is not just, because it is directed, not to the common good, but to the private good of the ruler, as the Philosopher states. Consequently there is no sedition in disturbing a government of this kind, unless indeed the tyrant's rule be disturbed so inordinately that his subjects suffer greater harm from the consequent disturbance than from the tyrant's government.",
    },
    meditation: "Thomas treats the tyrant with the cool gaze of a clinician. He is not a polemicist. He is reading Aristotle, and he is also reading the long Christian memory of bad kings, and he is trying to give the Christian thinker — including the Christian advocate, though he did not use that word — a vocabulary precise enough to act on.\n\nThe tyrant, for Aquinas, is not defined by cruelty. He is defined by orientation. A government is tyrannical when it is ordered not to the common good but to the private good of the ruler. This is a stunningly modern definition. It captures the autocrat who governs by decree, but it also captures the regulator captured by the industry he regulates, the prosecutor whose office is a launching pad for elected office, the bench packed to entrench a party, the corporate board whose duty to shareholders has been quietly redirected to the management's stock options. Tyranny in Aquinas's sense is not a temperament. It is a misdirection of the office.\n\nNotice the second sentence. There is no sedition in disturbing a tyrannical government — unless the disturbance is worse than the tyranny. Thomas refuses both the courtier's quietism and the revolutionary's enthusiasm. He says: the question is consequential. What happens after? Will the people suffer more from the upheaval than from the abuse? In the Summa proper (II-II q.42 a.2 ad 3) he is even sharper: it is the tyrant who is the seditious one, because it is he who has broken the peace of the community by ordering it to himself. Resisting him is not the disturbance. He is the disturbance.\n\nFor a lawyer, this matters because the lawyer is rarely asked to overthrow anyone. The lawyer is asked, much more often, to participate in small tyrannies. The agency that has stopped reading its statute. The judge who has stopped reading the record. The senior partner who has stopped reading the file and now signs whatever the associate puts in front of him. Each is a local misdirection of an office. Each is, in the technical Thomistic sense, tyrannical in miniature.\n\nThomas's counsel is sober. He does not say resist always, and he does not say comply always. He says weigh the consequences honestly, and remember whose disturbance it really is. The lawyer who refuses to file a brief that misstates the record has not disturbed the court. The court was already disturbed; the lawyer is the first one to notice aloud. The associate who tells the partner that the deal cannot close on these representations has not disturbed the deal. The deal was already broken; she is the witness, not the breaker.\n\nRead 1 Samuel carefully. The people demand a king, and Samuel, on the Lord's instruction, warns them what kings do. He will take your sons. He will take your daughters. He will take the tenth, and the best, and you will be his servants. The text is not anti-monarchical so much as anti-illusion. Earthly authority, even when legitimate, has an appetite. The Christian lawyer who works for the state, or for a great corporation, or for a powerful client, should not be surprised when the appetite shows itself. Samuel does not tell the people to refuse a king. He tells them what they are buying. Then he lets them buy it.\n\nWhat this clarity protects you from is the worst form of cynicism. The lawyer who has not read Samuel believes either that the king is good (and is shocked when he is not) or that all kings are bad (and so resigns into uselessness). The lawyer who has read Samuel knows what kings do, serves them within the limits of justice, and walks away when those limits are crossed. The mind is not scandalized, because it was warned. The hands are not soiled, because they knew where the dirt was.\n\nThere is a passage in De Regno that the textbooks sometimes flatten. Thomas, having considered the temptation to tyrannicide, declines to bless it. Private persons, he says, cannot kill the tyrant; the matter is for public authority, and even then with great caution, because the cure may be worse than the disease. This is not weakness. It is the Christian refusal to vest the conscience of one man with the power of revolution. The lawyer's analog is the refusal to make his own indignation the unit of action. You may know the agency is captured. You may not, on that knowledge alone, file a brief that lies. The cure must be lawful, public, and proportionate.\n\nSo what is left for you? More than you think. You can refuse to write the false memo. You can document, in calendar entries and contemporaneous notes, what was said and when. You can take the issue to ethics counsel, and if there is no ethics counsel, you can take it to the bar. You can decline the next matter from the client who asked you to lie. You can resign. None of these are sedition. They are the small lawful disturbances that, multiplied across a profession, hold a regime to the four marks of law that Thomas gave you yesterday.\n\nThe tyrant fears the lawyer who has read Aquinas because such a lawyer is hard to flatter and impossible to confuse. He knows what an office is for. He knows what a sedition is and is not. He has the words for what is happening, and the words are older than the regime.",
